## REST endpoint

### Registration

Namespace: `selecta/v1`
Route: `GET /wp-json/selecta/v1/search`
Permission callback: `__return_true` (public read, no auth required)
Register on: `rest_api_init`

### Query parameter `q`

- Required.
- Process in the correct WordPress order: unslash first, then sanitize. Use `sanitize_text_field( wp_unslash( $raw_query ) )`. Never sanitize a slashed value.
- If missing, empty, or fewer than 3 characters: return empty results array immediately, do not run a database query.
- Length check must use `mb_strlen( trim( $query ), 'UTF-8' ) < 3`. Do not use `strlen()` — Bulgarian/Cyrillic characters are multi-byte and `strlen()` counts bytes, not characters. A 2-character Cyrillic query has `strlen()` = 4, so `strlen() < 3` evaluates to false and the query incorrectly passes the minimum-length guard. `mb_strlen()` returns 2 for the same input, so `mb_strlen() < 3` correctly blocks it.

### WP_Query rules

- `post_type`: `selecta_product`
- `post_status`: `publish`
- `posts_per_page`: `10` — hard limit, never higher
- `no_found_rows`: `true` — skip total count for performance
- `update_post_meta_cache`: `false`
- `update_post_term_cache`: `false`
- Use a `meta_query` against `_selecta_search_index` with `'compare' => 'LIKE'`. Pass the plain sanitized query as the `value` — do not add `%` wildcards yourself and do not call `$wpdb->esc_like()` on the value. WordPress's `WP_Meta_Query` internally wraps the value in `'%' . $wpdb->esc_like( $value ) . '%'` for `LIKE` comparisons. Adding your own wildcards can cause the wildcards to be escaped or duplicated incorrectly by `WP_Meta_Query`. Use `CHAR` cast.
- Also match the post title. If doing this via a `posts_clauses` filter with a raw `$wpdb->prepare()` call, that is a different code path — there you must call `$wpdb->esc_like()` yourself and add the wildcards manually because `$wpdb->prepare()` does not do this automatically. Keep the implementation simple; a secondary title-only `WP_Query` pass is acceptable.
- If using a secondary title query, merge the results in priority order: title matches first, then index matches. Deduplicate by post ID before returning — a product that matches both queries must appear only once. After deduplication, slice the combined array to a maximum of 10 items. Never return more than 10 products regardless of how many queries were used internally.
- After the query, call `wp_reset_postdata()`.

### Response shape

Return a JSON array. Each element:

```json
{
  "id": 123,
  "title": "Product title",
  "url": "https://example.com/products/product-slug/",
  "line": "Boucles",
  "benefit": "Хидратира и дефинира",
  "score": 4.5,
  "price": "29,90 лв.",
  "image_url": "https://example.com/wp-content/uploads/...",
  "image_alt": "Product title"
}
```

- `line`: first term name from `selecta_product_line`, or empty string.
- `benefit`: value of `product_short_benefits` ACF field, first line only (split on newline, take index 0), or empty string.
- `score`: numeric `product_score` ACF value clamped 0–5, or `null` if unset/non-numeric.
- `price`: trimmed `product_price` ACF string, or empty string if unset.
- `image_url` / `image_alt`: from `wp_get_attachment_image_src()` on the featured image at `medium` size. If no featured image, `image_url` is empty string.
- `title` and `image_alt`: decode HTML entities before output.
- Return an empty array `[]` when there are no results, never `null` or `false`.
- Do not include stock, cart, checkout, badge, SKU, or any commerce-action field in the response.

## Search index meta field

### Key

`_selecta_search_index`

### What it is

A single plain-text string per product. Concatenation of all searchable fields with spaces between them. Used by the REST endpoint's `meta_query`.

### Builder function

Name: `selecta_build_search_index( $post_id ): void`

Do not add a strict `int` type hint on `$post_id`. The ACF `acf/save_post` hook may pass the post ID as a string or a non-post value such as `'options'`. A strict scalar type hint would throw a `TypeError` in that case. Inside the function, first check `is_numeric( $post_id )` and return early if false, then convert with `$post_id = absint( $post_id )` and return early if the result is zero. Use `absint()` rather than a raw `(int)` cast — it is the WordPress-idiomatic way to sanitize a post ID.

Location: `inc/search.php`

#### Guards — return early if any of these are true

- `! is_numeric( $post_id )` — return early; the ACF `acf/save_post` hook can fire with non-numeric values such as `'options'`.
- After the numeric check, convert with `absint()` and return early if the result is zero.
- `defined( 'DOING_AUTOSAVE' ) && DOING_AUTOSAVE`
- `wp_is_post_revision( $post_id )`
- `get_post_type( $post_id ) !== 'selecta_product'`
- `get_post_status( $post_id ) === 'trash'`

#### Fields to index — in this order

The order below is the concatenation order inside the index string. It does NOT create search ranking or scoring. The REST endpoint uses a `LIKE` query which matches anywhere in the string with equal weight regardless of position. The order is documented here only so the index is built consistently and can be read predictably in the database.

| Order | Source | How to get it |
|-------|--------|---------------|
| 1 | Post title | `get_the_title( $post_id )` |
| 2 | `selecta_product_line` terms | `wp_get_object_terms( $post_id, 'selecta_product_line', ['fields' => 'names'] )` |
| 3 | `selecta_product_category` terms | `wp_get_object_terms( $post_id, 'selecta_product_category', ['fields' => 'names'] )` |
| 4 | `selecta_hair_concern` terms | `wp_get_object_terms( $post_id, 'selecta_hair_concern', ['fields' => 'names'] )` |
| 5 | `selecta_hair_type` terms | `wp_get_object_terms( $post_id, 'selecta_hair_type', ['fields' => 'names'] )` |
| 6 | `product_short_benefits` | `get_field( 'product_short_benefits', $post_id )` — ACF textarea |
| 7 | `product_badge` | `get_field( 'product_badge', $post_id )` — ACF text |
| 8 | `product_sections` body_text | Loop `have_rows( 'product_sections', $post_id )`. For each row where `get_row_layout() === 'product_description'`, get `get_sub_field( 'body_text' )` and strip HTML with `wp_strip_all_tags()`. |

#### Fields explicitly excluded from the index

- `product_price` — not searchable; display-only in results
- `product_score` — not searchable; display-only in results
- `product_hover_image` — binary asset reference
- Any ACF image or file field
- Post date, post author, slug

#### Build steps

1. Collect all values into a flat PHP array of strings.
2. For each value: check it is a non-empty string. Skip `WP_Error` returns from taxonomy queries.
3. For any value containing HTML: run `wp_strip_all_tags()` before adding to the array.
4. Implode with a single space: `implode( ' ', array_filter( $parts ) )`.
5. Save: `update_post_meta( $post_id, '_selecta_search_index', sanitize_textarea_field( $index_string ) )`.

#### Hooks to trigger the builder

Hook 1 — native WordPress save:
```
add_action( 'save_post_selecta_product', 'selecta_build_search_index', 20 );
```

Hook 2 — ACF save (covers field-group saves that bypass `save_post`):
```
add_action( 'acf/save_post', 'selecta_build_search_index', 20 );
```
Guard inside the function handles the post-type check so the ACF hook is safe to fire broadly.

#### Bulk rebuild for existing products

Products only get indexed on save. After first deploy, run a one-time rebuild for all existing `selecta_product` posts (WP-CLI loop or admin action) so pre-existing products are searchable.

#### Future excerpt support

The CPT currently supports `title`, `thumbnail`, `revisions` only — no `editor`, no `excerpt`.
If `excerpt` support is added to the CPT later, add `get_the_excerpt( $post_id )` to the index builder at priority Medium (between `product_short_benefits` and `product_badge`).
No other change is needed.

## JavaScript behaviour

File: `assets/js/search.js` — self-contained IIFE, enqueued as `selecta-search` with dependency on `selecta-main`.
Use vanilla JS only. No jQuery, no fetch polyfill (modern browsers only). Do not append search logic to `main.js`.

### Constants — read from `window.selectaSearch` (localized on `selecta-main`)

```
MIN_CHARS    = 3
DEBOUNCE_MS  = 250
REST_URL     = window.selectaSearch.restUrl
```

### Overlay open/close

Visibility is controlled by CSS classes, not the HTML `hidden` attribute. Same pattern as `mobile-menu`.

- Closed default: no `is-open` / `is-closing` classes; root has `aria-hidden="true"`.
- Search button click: add `is-open`, remove `is-closing`, set `aria-hidden="false"`, set `aria-expanded="true"` on button, lock body scroll, focus search input.
- Close button / Escape / backdrop click: remove `is-open`, add `is-closing`, set `aria-expanded="false"`, unlock body scroll.
- **Desktop (≥1025px):** remove `is-closing` immediately, set `aria-hidden="true"`, return focus to search button.
- **Mobile (≤1024px):** wait for `animationend` on `.search-overlay__panel`, then remove `is-closing`, set `aria-hidden="true"`, return focus to search button.
- Closing does not clear the input or results (state is preserved between opens).

### Input handling

- Listen to `input` event on the search field.
- Debounce: wait `DEBOUNCE_MS` (250ms) after the last keystroke before acting.
- If trimmed value is fewer than `MIN_CHARS` (3) characters: clear results list, hide loading and empty states. Do not fetch.
- If trimmed value is `MIN_CHARS` or more: fetch from `REST_URL`.

### Fetch

- `GET` request: `REST_URL + '?q=' + encodeURIComponent( trimmedQuery )`
- Show loading state before fetch.
- On success: hide loading state, render results or empty state.
- On error: hide loading state. Do not surface the error message to the user.
- Ignore responses where the query has already changed (stale response guard: compare query string before rendering).

### Render

- Clear the results `<ul>` before rendering.
- Max 10 items (server enforces this; JS renders whatever the server returns, up to 10).
- Show result count in `#search-count` when results exist.
- Each result: `<li>` containing `<a href="{url}">` with:
  - Image `<img>` or placeholder `<span>` if no image.
  - Line label `<span>` — omitted if empty.
  - Title `<span>`.
  - Benefit `<span>` — omitted if empty.
  - Stars row — only if `score` is a valid number (uses `#selecta-search-star-template` markup from PHP).
  - Price `<span>` — only if `price` is non-empty.
  - Hover arrow (desktop only, hidden on mobile).
- Clicking a result closes the overlay.
- No add-to-cart. No badge in results.
- Never use `innerHTML` to render any value that comes from the REST API response (title, line, benefit, URL, image alt, price, etc.). Use `textContent` for text. Use `setAttribute` or direct property assignment for `href`, `src`, `alt`. Create elements with `document.createElement()`. Star SVG markup from the hardcoded PHP `<template>` is the only allowed `insertAdjacentHTML` source.

### ARIA

- `aria-expanded` on the search button reflects open/closed state.
- `aria-hidden` on `#search-overlay` toggled by JS: `true` when closed, `false` when open.
- `aria-busy="true"` on `#search-results` while a fetch is pending.
- `aria-busy` removed when fetch completes (success or error).
- Overlay has `role="dialog"`, `aria-modal="true"`, `aria-label`.

### Focus trap

- While the overlay is open, Tab and Shift+Tab must cycle between: search input, result links, close button.
- Focus must not escape to the page behind the overlay.

## Mobile drawer animation

Below `64rem` (1024px), the search overlay uses the same open/close animation pattern as `mobile-menu`:

- Root: `display: block; position: fixed; inset: 0; pointer-events: none` — visible in DOM always, not hidden via `[hidden]`.
- `is-open` / `is-closing`: `pointer-events: auto`.
- Backdrop: fade in/out keyframes (`searchOverlayFadeIn` / `searchOverlayFadeOut`).
- Panel (`.search-overlay__panel`): `position: absolute; bottom: 0; left: 0; right: 0; transform: translateY(100%)` — slide up/down keyframes (`searchDrawerSlideIn` / `searchDrawerSlideOut`).
- Do not use `prefers-reduced-motion` overrides that disable drawer animations unless the mobile menu also gets them — keep both drawers consistent.

## PHP template: search-overlay.php

File: `template-parts/components/search-overlay.php`

Include once in `header.php` after `</header>`, before `mobile-menu.php`.

Required elements:
- Outer: `<div id="search-overlay" class="search-overlay" role="dialog" aria-modal="true" aria-label="..." aria-hidden="true">` — no `hidden` attribute.
- Backdrop: `.search-overlay__backdrop`
- Panel: `.search-overlay__panel` with `.search-overlay__drawer-top` (mobile white strip), header bar, body
- Search input: `type="search"`, `id="search-input"`, `autocomplete="off"`, `spellcheck="false"`
- Close button: `type="button"`, `id="search-close"`, keyboard accessible
- Results count: `<p id="search-count" class="search-overlay__count" hidden>`
- Results list: `<ul id="search-results" class="search-overlay__results" aria-live="polite" aria-atomic="false">`. Do not use `role="listbox"`.
- Loading state: `<p id="search-loading" class="search-overlay__loading" hidden>`
- Empty state: `<p id="search-no-results" class="search-overlay__empty" hidden>`
- Star template: `<template id="selecta-search-star-template">` with `selecta_get_svg( 'star2' )`

No product data is hard-coded in this template. All results are rendered by JS.

## Search button in site-header-bar.php

The existing button has class `site-header__action--search`.
Required attributes:
- `aria-expanded="false"`
- `aria-controls="search-overlay"`

## wp_localize_script and enqueue in assets.php

`selectaSearch` is localized on `selecta-main` (available before `selecta-search` loads):

```php
wp_localize_script(
    'selecta-main',
    'selectaSearch',
    array(
        'restUrl'    => esc_url_raw( rest_url( 'selecta/v1/search' ) ),
        'minChars'   => 3,
        'debounceMs' => 250,
    )
);

wp_enqueue_script(
    'selecta-search',
    $search_js_uri,
    array( 'selecta-main' ),
    filemtime( $search_js_path ),
    true
);
```

CSS handle `selecta-search-overlay` is registered in `selecta_register_component_styles()` and enqueued from `search-overlay.php` via `wp_enqueue_style( 'selecta-search-overlay' )`.

